PATCH 549: the side unit counters DGROUP:0x6d38..0x6d3b are four bytes, and their pair stores are words.

FUN_1000_a5dc decides victory and defeat from these counters:

    1a678: 80 3e ab 6d 00    cmpb  $0x0,0x6dab       ; not in the editor
    1a67d: 75 3b             jne   0x1a6ba
    1a67f: 80 3e 3a 6d 00    cmpb  $0x0,0x6d3a       ; own side ever had units?
    1a684: 74 34             je    0x1a6ba
    1a686: 80 3e 38 6d 00    cmpb  $0x0,0x6d38       ; own side wiped?
    1a68b: 74 2e             je    0x1a6bb            ; -> outcome 1 (DEFEAT)
    1a68d: 83 3e 90 97 00    cmpw  $0x0,-0x6870       ; opposing set ever had units?
    1a692: 74 26             je    0x1a6ba
    1a694: 83 3e 8e 97 00    cmpw  $0x0,-0x6872       ; opposing set wiped?
    1a699: 75 1f             jne   0x1a6ba
    ...
    1a6ae: c7 06 a0 6d 00 00 movw  $0x0,0x6da0        ; -> outcome 0 (VICTORY)

The census task FUN_1000_6049 (asm 0x16049, rebuilt by patch 264) fills them.  It counts two halves of
the 32-entry cell-index table at DGROUP:0x6d3c into two register halves and commits them like this:

    16083: 8b c8             mov   %ax,%cx
    16085: 89 0e 38 6d       mov   %cx,0x6d38         ; CL -> 0x6d38 live A, CH -> 0x6d39 live B
    16089: 3a 0e 3a 6d       cmp   0x6d3a,%cl
    1608d: 76 04             jbe   0x16093
    1608f: 88 0e 3a 6d       mov   %cl,0x6d3a         ; peak A
    16093: 3a 2e 3b 6d       cmp   0x6d3b,%ch
    16097: 76 04             jbe   0x1609d
    16099: 88 2e 3b 6d       mov   %ch,0x6d3b         ; peak B

So the layout is four bytes -- live A, live B, peak A, peak B -- written as one word plus two byte
stores, and read back only by byte (`cmpb` at 0x1a67f/0x1a686, `movzbw` at 0x162ba/0x1bb05).  There is
no 32-bit access to either address anywhere in the image.

Two width errors sat on top of that:

  - `DAT_2000_2d38`, `DAT_2000_2d3a` and `DAT_2000_2d3b` were `undefined2`, so each scalar test covered
    TWO bytes.  `DAT_2000_2d38 == 0` therefore demanded that BOTH sides be at zero, and the defeat
    outcome at 0x1a6bb -- the one that fires when the OWN side is destroyed while the enemy survives --
    was unreachable in exactly the case it exists for.  `DAT_2000_2d3b = ...` also wrote 0x6d3c.
  - `_DAT_2000_2d38` and `_DAT_2000_2d3a` were `undefined4`, so each pair store wrote four bytes.  The
    asm is `movw`: 0x4423 `movw $0x0,0x6d38`, 0x4429 `movw $0x0,0x6d3a`, 0x16085 `mov %cx,0x6d38`.
    `_DAT_2000_2d3a = 0` at mission init therefore cleared 0x6d3a..0x6d3d, wiping the first entry of
    the very cell-index table at 0x6d3c that FUN_1000_6049 then walks.

Note that 0x6d3a and 0x6d3c also serve as BIASED table bases elsewhere (`mov 0x6d3a(%bx),%si` /
`mov %si,0x6d3c(%bx)` at 0x1a9cb/0x1a9fc, with `bx = ((byte[di+0x1b] << 2) + byte[di+0x1c]) << 1`).
That is not a conflict: the index is at least 8, so those accesses never touch the four counter bytes.

Only the five declarations change; every expression that uses them is already what the asm says.

board:0017

